276
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
perspective and he needs

277
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
determination.

278
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
He needs a clear understanding of

279
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
the aims and methods of Scouting.

280
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
and perspective is seeing things

281
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
in, measuring progress from a

282
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
boy's viewpoint, from a

283
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
boy's scale, and determination

284
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
is kind of sticking with the

285
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
consistent application of the

286
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
vision and the

287
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
perspective.

288
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
I think, Shane, that

289
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
you've got the vision-

290
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
I mean I'm sure of that-

291
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
And I think you have the

292
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
determination.

293
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
So let's work on the perspective

294
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:39,000
a little bit.

295
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Maybe that'll help you out.

296
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
You probably have

297
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
some kind of a really idealized

298
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
picture or idea

299
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
of a boy-led troop.

300
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Now

301
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
I'm saying that because I started

302
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
with that kind of

303
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
a picture in mind as a young

304
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,000
Scoutmaster.

305
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
That picture was based pretty much

306
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:00,000
on the first edition of the

307
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Patrol Leaders Handbook.

308
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
I had a copy of that as a boy.

309
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
I was never a Scout as a boy,

310
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
but I had that book

311
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
And in that book all the boys,

312
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
they wore perfect uniforms

313
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
and set up perfect campsites.

314
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
There were no photographs

315
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
in the book, It was just

316
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
drawings, right,

317
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
And so they were further idealized.

318
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
You know, the Patrol Leader said

319
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
things like: come on, fellows, I think

320
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
this is a great place for a hike.

321
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
And that idealized

322
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
look at Scouting

323
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
really stuck with me And I tried to make it

324
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
real as a young Scoutmaster. but

325
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
I learned after a few years

326
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
that I was missing the forest

327
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
for the trees. There was no such thing

328
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
as the Patrol. that was in that book.

329
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
I was also laboring under

330
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
the mishaprehension that a Scoutmaster

331
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
was in charge and responsible to instruct

332
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
and lead the Scouts. Now

333
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
it was really great fun for me to lead.

334
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
I had a great time with it

335
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
And I learned a lot

336
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
And I was happy

337
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
and my Scouts were happy and we had a great troop.

338
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
I realized, though,

339
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
that all the fun and personal growth

340
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
I was experiencing as a Scoutmaster

341
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
was never really intended for me.

342
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
It was intended

343
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
for the Scouts. So I stopped

344
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
doing what they should be doing

345
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
and started helping them do it themselves.

346
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
and, long story short, you know

347
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
that's the way that it worked.

348
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
They weren't doing

349
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
things as well as I could-

350
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
not by a long shot.

351
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
I think the most important aspect

352
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
of this perspective

353
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
that we're trying to look at

354
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
is that quality of instruction,

355
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
quality of leadership,

356
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
the slickness of the program-

357
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
that's not the outcome

358
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
of Scouting. If you get

359
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
those things, that's a fringe benefit.

360
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
Scouting

361
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
is the process of boys doing things

362
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
that matter.

363
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
That's what's really

364
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
at the heart of things. When boys

365
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
do things, they do things imperfectly

366
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
and chaotically from an adult perspective,

367
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
but they are doing it,

368
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
and that's really what matters.

369
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
This is worth repeating.

370
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
Boys doing the process

371
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
of Scouting is all that really matters.

372
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
the quality of their leadership,

373
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
their planning and skill

374
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
is not what really matters.

375
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
I get a fair number of emails from Scout Leaders

376
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
who truly want to transition

377
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
into a boy led model and think this will take

378
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
months and months and I just don't think it's so.

379
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
You have a boy led troop

380
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
and patrols. when the boys

381
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
lead their troop and patrols,

382
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
it doesn't matter how well

383
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
they lead, it's that they are

384
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
in the process of Scouting.

385
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Now this thought

386
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
scares the heck out of adults,

387
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
especially ones with a troop full of younger Scouts.

388
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
They think the process

389
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
might work. they try it out

390
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
for a few weeks and the results

391
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
are so uncertain and chaotic that

392
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
they quickly revert to the adult

393
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
led version again and they think

394
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
the guy online

395
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
is crazy.

396
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
What they are missing

397
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
is that the uncertainty and the chaos

398
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
is actually what we want to see.

399
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
It's proof that the process

400
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
is really happening.

401
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
Now you may feel that you

402
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
can't put the planning

403
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
and instruction and leadership

404
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
for your troop

405
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
in the hands of your boys,

406
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
but you really can

407
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
and you really must.

408
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
As time goes by you'll see them

409
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
get a little better at it and within a few years

410
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
you'll be a bit closer to what you've envisioned,

411
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
but only through determination

412
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
to stick with the vision.

413
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
Your tools are coaching and mentoring

414
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and asking lots of questions.

415
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
How many times have I said that

416
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
You have to fully concentrate

417
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
your efforts on asking lots of questions

418
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
and train your Scouts in that way

419
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
to think for themselves.

420
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Here's an idea.

421
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
Here's an illustration.

422
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
How would you introduce a bunch of boys to the game of basketball?

423
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
Would you sit them all down

424
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
and start reading the NCAA rulebook

425
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
to them?

426
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
Nah, you wouldn't do that.

427
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
They wouldn't stand for it, would they?

428
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
Now you'd start with the ball

429
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
and the basket,

430
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
Say: here's a ball, here's a basket.

431
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
What happens next? I don't know

432
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
How, about putting the ball in the basket? Oh,

433
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
They'd try a few shots.

434
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
Maybe they'd make some, or maybe they wouldn't,

435
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
But they'd be pretty fascinated with the idea, wouldn't they?

436
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
And then you could introduce the idea

437
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
of dribbling. Hey, you know,

438
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
when you walk with the ball, you have to dribble it.

439
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
Oh, really, Yeah, that's how it works.

440
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Oh, okay,

441
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
Let them try that out for a while.

442
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
And after they've been dribbling

443
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
and going up and down the court,

444
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
they might need to learn a little bit about the double dribble,

445
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
about traveling.

446
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
You could introduce the idea of two teams

447
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
and then rules

448
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
for offensive and defensive play.

449
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
So what you'd be doing is moving from some

450
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
very simple concepts

451
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
to more complex ones

452
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
Over time.

453
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
you'd have a team that could play other teams

454
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
that maybe win a game or two,

455
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
but it would be a while before they really

456
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
understood the game,

457
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Before they really appreciated what was going on.

458
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
So

459
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
you start a team. it takes a while to get them

460
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
to the point where they can play

461
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
and they get beat every time.

462
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
So here's another piece of perspective.

463
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
Why are they playing the game?

464
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
Just to pass time? just to learn

465
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
something new,

466
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
to become, you know, national champions?

467
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
No, they're playing the game

468
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
because the act of playing itself

469
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
is sufficient for them to reap rewards.

470
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
They're playing the game

471
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
because the act of playing

472
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
itself is sufficient for them

473
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
to get something out of it.

474
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
I mean, at least they're going to get

475
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
a little more physically fit.

476
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
if they're playing a game of basketball,

477
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Maybe they'll learn a little bit about strategy

478
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and teamwork,

479
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
Maybe they'll learn a little bit about leadership.

480
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
Playing the game

481
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
is in itself

482
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
sufficient for them to reap

483
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
rewards.

484
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
Scouting is exactly the same.

485
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
It's not

486
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
focused on outdoor skills,

487
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
It's not focused on

488
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
slick, military-like precision,

489
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
It's not focused

490
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
on anything but

491
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
the process,

492
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
And boys who go through the process

493
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
of scouting gain advantages

494
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
that they may not understand or appreciate

495
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
at the time, that their parents

496
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
may not understand or appreciate

497
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
at the time, but they're going to get

498
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
something out of it. Another piece

499
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
of that boy perspective

500
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
is the amount of information they can

501
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
effectively handle at any given time.

502
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
Like I said,

503
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
I wouldn't teach boys to play basketball

504
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
by starting out with the NCAA

505
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
Rule Book, which is a pretty

506
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
thick book, by the way.

507
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
I mean,

508
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
you could set up an all-day event

509
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
where you're going to read it to them

510
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
and maybe you'll make a PowerPoint presentation

511
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
and you know,

512
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
but they're going to check out

513
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
after the first half hour.

514
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
No matter how excited and motivated they are,

515
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
they're going to check out.

516
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
I think the better approach,

517
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
especially from the

518
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
boy perspective-

519
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
short periods of instruction

520
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
followed by long periods of hands-on

521
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
practice. that seems to

522
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
be what works, especially with younger

523
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
scouts. So that's a piece

524
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
of the perspective that I would encourage you to think about.

525
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
So, Shane, let's take all this and we'll

526
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
put it in really practical terms. One:

527
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
stop leading and instructing,

528
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
Just stop doing it.

529
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
Number two: ask

530
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
your scouts what they want to do next.

531
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
Get to your next meeting, say, hey, guys,

532
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
you know I had some things planned, but

533
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
what would you like to do next?

534
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
And

535
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
then ask them how they think

536
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
that whatever they want to do will

537
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
happen. Ask them who will make

538
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
it happen. Ask them what they think

539
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
you could do to help

540
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
them make it happen. maybe Ask

541
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
them what they're going to need, ask them how they're

542
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
going to plan it. ask, ask, ask,

543
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
ask. just keep the questions coming.

544
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
Don't talk them to death.

545
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
Give them a chance to act and do

546
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
what they're going to do. Observe

547
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
that and encourage

548
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
the least expression of initiative

549
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
and skill, Just even the

550
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
little glimmer in the dark

551
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
of initiative and skill,

552
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
and build on those expressions by asking

553
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
more questions. Hey, you seem

554
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
to be really good at that. You

555
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
really know how to do those knots.

556
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
Have you learned any new ones lately?

557
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
Do you know where you could find out how to

558
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
learn more? Have you

559
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
taught anybody how to do that? Does

